Title:
AntiVE-BehaviorWatch

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
AntiVE-BehaviorWatch is a Windows C research project that gates execution based on whether it detects real human interaction, rather than using classic anti-analysis checks (VM/sandbox/debugger/hardware fingerprinting). It embeds a GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) neural network and performs offline inference on real-time mouse telemetry to classify behavior as “human” vs “idle/artificial.” The tool extracts movement features (distance, velocity, acceleration, jerk, angles, axis deltas, idle streaks) across multiple observation phases and uses a majority-voting decision engine to decide whether to continue or abort execution. This explores a behavior-based anti-automation/anti-sandbox concept that can be used to resist automated detonation and scripted interaction. It’s most relevant to red teamers, malware analysts/researchers, and defenders studying emerging anti-analysis patterns and how to simulate or detect them. The interesting angle is the “embedded AI” approach: no external ML runtime, model weights compiled into the binary, and decisions made purely from interaction dynamics.

Technical Focus:
- Windows mouse telemetry collection and feature extraction
- Behavioral biometrics for human-vs-automation classification
- Embedded GRU neural network inference in C (offline, no runtime deps)
- Multi-phase sampling and majority-vote decision logic
- Execution gating / anti-analysis via interaction verification

Use Cases:
- Add human-presence checks to research implants or controlled red-team payloads
- Study and reproduce AI-based anti-sandbox/anti-automation techniques
- Develop defensive detections for binaries performing mouse-dynamics classification
- Build sandbox interaction simulators that mimic human mouse kinematics
- Benchmark behavioral features (jerk/angle/idle streaks) for bot vs human separation

Keywords:
Windows, C, GRU, Gated Recurrent Unit, embedded AI, offline inference, mouse dynamics, cursor velocity, acceleration, jerk, angular velocity, idle detection, behavioral biometrics, anti-analysis, sandbox evasion, automated detonation, execution gating, feature extraction, majority voting, model weights, telemetry